WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.110
Welcome to machine learning.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.110
What is machine learning?

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:12.110
You probably use it many times a day without even knowing it.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.110
Anytime you want to find out something like

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.110
how do I make a sushi roll,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.110
you can do a web search on Google Bing or Baidu to find out.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:24.110
And that works so well because their machine learning software

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.110
has figured out how to rank web pages.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.110
Or when you upload pictures to Instagram or Snapchat

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.110
and think to yourself,

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.110
I want to tag my friends so they can see their pictures.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.110
Well, these apps can recognize your friends in your pictures

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.110
and label them as well.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.110
That's also machine learning.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.110
Or if you've just finished watching a Star Wars movie

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.110
on a video streaming service and you think,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.110
what other similar movies could I watch?

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.110
Well, the streaming service will likely use machine learning

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.110
to recommend something that you might like.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:00.110
Each time you use voice-to-text on your phone to write a text message,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.110
or tell your phone,

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.110
hey Siri, play a song by Rihanna,

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.110
or ask your other phone,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.110
okay Google, show me Indian restaurants near me.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.110
That's also machine learning.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.110
Each time you receive an email titled,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.110
congratulations, you've won a million dollars.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.110
Well, maybe you're rich, congratulations.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.110
Or more likely your email service will probably flag it as spam.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.110
That too is an application of machine learning.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.110
Beyond consumer applications that you might use,

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.110
AI is also rapidly making its way into big companies

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.110
and into industrial applications.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.110
For example, I'm deeply concerned about climate change

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.110
and I'm glad to see that machine learning is already hoping

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.110
to optimize wind turbine power generation.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.110
Or in healthcare, it's starting to make its way into hospitals

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.110
to help doctors make accurate diagnoses.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.110
Or recently at LearningAI, I've been doing a lot of work

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:03.110
putting computer vision into factories

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.110
to help inspect if something coming off the assembly line

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.110
has any defects.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.110
That's machine learning.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.110
It's the science of getting computers to learn

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.110
without being explicitly programmed.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.110
In this class, you learn about machine learning

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.110
and get to implement machine learning in code yourself.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.110
Millions of others have taken the earlier version of this course

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.110
which is a course that led to the founding of Coursera.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.110
And many learners ended up building exciting machine learning systems

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.110
or even pursuing very successful careers in AI.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.110
I'm excited that you're on this journey with me.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.110
Welcome and let's get started.
